---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Pipelines and the DAG designer — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-indigo-on-dark">For Developers</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">Pipelines and the DAG designer</h1>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
            <h2>Draft versus publish</h2>
            <p>
                Create a pipeline and edit steps, SQL, parameters, and edges on the
                draft. The DAG designer is a visual graph editor (drag/drop, connections,
                auto-layout, zoom, inspector). Saves debounce after idle and also honor
                Ctrl+S. Cycle detection runs before edges persist — an invalid graph
                does not save.
            </p>
            <p>
                Publish freezes an immutable version: SQL, edges, layouts, retry
                defaults, and failure policy together. Further edits go to a new draft
                or a new version. You never deploy a live draft. A published version
                opens read-only in the designer.
            </p>

            <h2>Edges</h2>
            <p>
                In the designer and in package YAML, <code class="text-foreground">to</code> depends on
                <code class="text-foreground">from</code>. Fan-out and fan-in are supported.
                Execution order is derived from the graph, not from a handwritten serial list.
                Legacy ordered pipelines import as sequential edges so older designs still run
                after DAG execution is enabled.
            </p>
            <p>
                Fan-in is not a new canvas shape. The consumer step’s
                <strong class="text-foreground">Join</strong> control chooses Wait all
                (every parent) or Wait any (first satisfied parent). Wait any is
                control-only: it is rejected on Artifact SQL, Staged Database SQL,
                <code class="text-foreground">from_step</code>, and
                <code class="text-foreground">artifact_available</code> edges. Full
                contract:
                <a href="/docs/developer-join-mode" class="text-link hover:underline">Wait all and Wait any</a>.
            </p>

            <h2>Structural roots</h2>
            <p>
                A step is a start node if and only if it has no incoming edges. There is
                no <code class="text-foreground">is_start</code> flag and no trigger-time
                “run from step X”. Publish warns when root count is greater than one and
                requires explicit confirmation. The designer and run visualizer badge
                those roots. Run plans snapshot <code class="text-foreground">root_step_codes</code>
                at create time.
            </p>

            <h2>Failure policy</h2>
            <p>
                Pipeline draft/version stores a required default
                <code class="text-foreground">dag_failure_policy</code>:
                <code class="text-foreground">fail_fast</code> or
                <code class="text-foreground">continue_independent</code>. A deployment may
                set an optional override; override wins when the run plan is created and
                is snapshotted. Only a step whose snapshotted step policy is
                <code class="text-foreground">fail_pipeline</code> trips the run-wide
                fail-fast latch. A step with <code class="text-foreground">continue</code>
                that fails skips dependents that require success; it does not poison
                independent branches. Skipped, cancelled, and abandoned terminals never
                trip fail-fast.
            </p>
            <p>
                In-run retry is a separate policy on the pipeline and each step
                (defaults: one attempt, writes unsafe). It does not replace failure
                policy. Details:
                <a href="/docs/developer-retry" class="text-link hover:underline">Step retry policies</a>.
            </p>

            <h2>Step create/edit</h2>
            <p>
                Metadata stays in Bootstrap modals. SQL may expand into Monaco and
                “Open in SQL Editor” without turning the whole designer into a full page.
                Sync hidden fields before submit. Uniqueness that would otherwise surface
                as an opaque unique violation is pre-checked.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
